 resolved 1 week ago, WBC normalization expected", "Gastrointestinal bleeding: Hgb 12 g/dL is mild and not acute enough for this pattern"], "alarm_features": ["Weight loss", "Elevated leukocyte count with normal differential", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.606299212598425, "posterior": 0.47495455764474515, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["A normal leukocyte differential (no neutrophilia or bandemia) is atypical for reactive leukocytosis (B3), favoring myeloid neoplasms like CML over benign cause…"], "evidence_against": ["The absence of splenomegaly or bone marrow infiltration signs reduces the likelihood of advanced myeloproliferative neoplasm (B1), as these are common physical…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.074, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP moderately supports CML, while weakly excluding other myeloproliferative neoplasms like PV, ET, and PMF."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.107, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["A normal leukocyte differential (no neutrophilia or bandemia) is atypical for reactive leukocytosis (B3), favoring myeloid neoplasms like CML over benign cause…"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Biliary Disease with Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.034, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4041994750656167, "posterior": 0.37170356685240924, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP moderately supports CML, while weakly excluding other myeloproliferative neoplasms like PV, ET, and PMF."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly weakly reduces CML likelihood but does not exclude it due to variable presentation in early disease."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Polycythemia Vera (PV)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.10104986876640418, "posterior": 0.061950594475401535, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Abdominal pain is weakly supportive of PV due to potential splenomegaly, but physical exam findings (no splenomegaly) limit this."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Essential Thrombocythemia (ET)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.021, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Low platelet count weakly excludes ET, as ET typically presents with thrombocytosis."]}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.021, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly and marrow signs weakly excludes PMF, which commonly presents with splenomegaly."]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.1", "B1.2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) strongly favor Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) over other myeloproliferative neoplasms?", "summary": "Elevated LAP moderately supports CML, while weakly excluding other myeloproliferative neoplasms like PV, ET, and PMF."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of abdominal pain align with Polycythemia Vera (PV) as a possible etiology?", "summary": "Abdominal pain is weakly supportive of PV due to potential splenomegaly, but physical exam findings (no splenomegaly) limit this."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly in the physical exam challenge the CML hypothesis?", "summary": "Absence of splenomegaly weakly reduces CML likelihood but does not exclude it due to variable presentation in early disease."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of polycythemia (normal hemoglobin) inconsistent with Polycythemia Vera (PV)?", "summary": "Normal hemoglobin is neutral for PV, as PV can present without overt polycythemia in early stages."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the low platelet count (177,000/mm³) inconsistent with Essential Thrombocythemia (ET)?", "summary": "Low platelet count weakly excludes ET, as ET typically presents with thrombocytosis."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly or bone marrow infiltration signs rule out Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) in this case?", "summary": "Absence of splenomegaly and marrow signs weakly excludes PMF, which commonly presents with splenomegaly."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.606], ["B1.1", 0.404], ["B2", 0.182]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.475], ["B1.1", 0.372], ["B3", 0.107]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Root posterior (0.606) < 0.75; B1.4 (danger 0.7, posterior 0.021) is a low-probability dangerous alternative. No info exhaustion (remaining value 0.9) and depth limit (max 3, current 2) not reached."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.9, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.37170356685240924, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'neoplasm', 'leukocytosis', 'Myelofibrosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.606}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)", "posterior": 0.372, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Absence of splenomegaly weakly reduces CML likelihood but does not exclude it due to variable presentation in early dis…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B2", "B1.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, burning sensation in extremities, confusion\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML): blast phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive, familial chronic myelocytic leukemia-like syndrome, leukemia, myeloid, accelerated-phase\n\nRelated to Polycythemia Vera (PV): acquired secondary polycythemia, congenital secondary polycythemia, polycythemia (disease)\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)"}
